    <div class="prose">
      <p class="manifesto-quote">“A place to record ideas and questions that may still matter after the noise of today has settled.”</p>

      <h2>Why 2075?</h2>
      <p>Because fifty years changes the question. It asks us to look beyond the next election, budget, or controversy and consider what kind of institutions, freedoms, opportunities, and public systems one generation should leave to the next.</p>
      <p>2075 is not a prediction and this site is not a master plan. It is a horizon—a discipline that helps separate durable questions from temporary noise.</p>
      <p>By 2075, I may no longer be here—or I will have lived most of the life available to me. That distance helps keep this exercise from becoming primarily about my own reward, relevance, or convenience. It asks what should endure for people I may never meet.</p>

      <h2>Why also look towards 2050?</h2>
      <p>2050 is the action horizon. It is close enough for reforms to become demands, programmes, experiments, and measurable institutional change within a working lifetime. 2075 remains the inheritance horizon: the longer view against which those nearer ambitions should be judged.</p>
      <p>An entry may belong to either horizon or to both. The site remains Vision for Bharat, 2075 because its governing discipline is still to think beyond the author’s immediate stake in the outcome.</p>

      <h2>Why “Bharat”?</h2>
      <p>Here, Bharat names the whole country in its variety: states and cities, villages and neighbourhoods, many languages, different histories, and a shared constitutional future. The word is used as an invitation to think at national scale without flattening regional life.</p>

      <h2>What this project is—and is not</h2>
      <div class="values">
        <div class="value"><h3>It is a journal</h3><p>A public record of ideas that can mature, be challenged, and change.</p></div>
        <div class="value"><h3>It is not a campaign</h3><p>It does not speak for a party, candidate, institution, or government.</p></div>
        <div class="value"><h3>It is personal</h3><p>I write when I have something worth preserving, without a fixed format or schedule.</p></div>
        <div class="value"><h3>It is fallible</h3><p>Ideas may be incomplete, change direction, or turn out to be wrong.</p></div>
      </div>

      <h2>National in ambition, grounded in place</h2>
      <p>Some work here addresses India as a whole. Other work begins with a state or locality where institutions can be observed more closely. Bihar is the first such lens because it is part of the author’s lived inheritance—not because one state should be mistaken for the country. State-level ideas remain in the same journal and can also test broader national principles.</p>

      <h2>A note on authorship</h2>
      <p>This first version keeps authorship deliberately minimal while the journal’s voice and standards take shape. Before public launch, this section should identify the author and disclose relevant experience, interests, and potential conflicts with enough clarity for readers to judge the work.</p>
